  "response": "The question of whether the Shroud of Turin is the first-century burial cloth of Jesus or a later artefact had, for most of the twentieth century, been argued from iconography, textile history and the optical properties of the image. Physical measurement entered the dispute decisively when radiocarbon dating of the Shroud of Turin was carried out in 1988. The procedure required removing linen from the relic itself. The samples for dating were cut from the shroud on 21 April 1988, taken as a single strip from one corner and subdivided under the observation of ecclesiastical authorities and scientific witnesses. The quantity of fabric consumed was small, yet that excision fixed the material on which every later argument would turn.\n\nThat 1988 dating programme resulted in three laboratories performing the radiocarbon tests on the shroud, at Oxford, Zurich and Tucson, each working on its own allocated portion with accelerator mass spectrometry. By contrast, four laboratories performed the 1988 radiocarbon tests on the shroud, a count that folds the coordinating institution into the analytical work. Control samples of known age accompanied the shroud fragments, so that each laboratory's procedure could be checked against textiles whose dates were already established, and the measurements were pooled only after independent analysis.\n\nThe 1988 tests yielded a calibrated date range of AD 1260 to 1390 at 95 percent confidence. That interval indicates that the 1988 radiocarbon results placed the origin of the shroud's linen in the Middle Ages, far from the first century required by the authenticity thesis. In other words, the 1988 tests dated the cloth of the shroud to the medieval period. The range also sits comfortably with the documentary record, since the cloth's first secure appearance, at Lirey in France, falls in the mid-fourteenth century.\n\nChemist Raymond Rogers published a 2005 paper arguing that the dated sample came from a repaired area, some seventeen years after Cardinal Anastasio Ballestrero announced the dating results in October 1988. Even though that objection struck at the integrity of the sampled material, the medieval date assigned to the cloth was left standing by it, because the objection disputed what had been measured rather than how it had been measured. The dispute over the sample thus reduces to a strict choice. Either the sample used in the 1988 dating was representative of the main cloth of the shroud, or the sample used in the 1988 dating was not representative of the main cloth of the shroud; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. The second horn, the corner's lack of representativeness, is precisely what confirms the Rogers paper's claim of a repaired area, since a patch woven into the original fabric would explain any divergence between that corner and the remainder of the linen.\n\nYet the repair hypothesis does not return the cloth to the first century. At least one of the following holds, perhaps both: the main linen of the shroud was woven in the medieval period, or the sampled corner of the shroud contained medieval repair material. Neither branch supports authenticity, because each locates medieval fibre in the very place the laboratories examined. The sampled corner's repair material stands, by contrast, beside the finding that the 1988 tests dated the shroud to between AD 1000 and 1100, an earlier medieval horizon that still lies more than nine centuries after the Crucifixion. Against this background, the 1989 Nature paper concluded that the results provide conclusive evidence that the linen of the Shroud of Turin is mediaeval, and that conclusion settles the tension between the repaired-area objection and the medieval dating: whatever the provenance of the corner, the published measurements support no first-century origin for the cloth.",
